# Required workflow

Each implementation unit follows this sequence:

1. ChatGPT authorizes a focused PR.
2. Cursor verifies exact main, branch and clean working tree.
3. Cursor reads the required records.
4. Cursor implements only approved scope.
5. Cursor runs focused tests while implementing.
6. Cursor performs adversarial self-review.
7. Cursor runs the full required suite.
8. Cursor opens or updates a draft PR.
9. Cursor records exact implementation and CI evidence.
10. Claude Code independently reviews the exact implementation head.
11. Cursor implements approved corrections.
12. Claude independently re-reviews.
13. ChatGPT makes the technical acceptance decision.
14. User explicitly authorizes merge. This remains the CURRENT merge
    rule. Proposed protocol §5 bounded merge delegation is INACTIVE and
    cannot replace this step.
15. PR is merged.
16. Project status is synchronized.
17. Only then may the next dependent PR begin. Separately authorized,
    dependency-independent lanes may run in parallel under AGENTS.md and
    ACCELERATED_SAFE_DELIVERY.md, with frozen shared contracts and exclusive
    ownership. This is not permission to start a future runtime phase.
    Future or dependent work remains gated.

Stop when:
- expected main or branch HEAD differs;
- the working tree is not clean before starting;
- the active phase records conflict;
- the work requires an unauthorized later or dependent PR;
- ownership or data must be guessed;
- production access is required;
- prohibited scope becomes necessary;
- required tests cannot be executed.


Session, branch and result rules are in
stocky-plus/docs/agents/PROPO_AUTONOMOUS_DELIVERY_PROTOCOL.md.
That protocol does not change the CURRENT user-only merge rule. Loading it
does not activate §5. Any future activation must amend this file together
with 00-project-governance.mdc, AGENTS.md, and protocol §5.
Reuse an open-PR chat only when agent, role, task, branch, PR, objective and
session ownership still match. A review is never an author-chat continuation.
